d on the  front end  for what the second and third chance  looks like it's very much like a parent  isn't it which is when a child is young  which is ostensibly their first chance  at doing most things that they're gonna  do everything  not most things everything they're not  gonna ride a bicycle for the first time  properly they're not going to  use their knife and fork properly it's  all going to be a mess  it's not so much that we put a date on  it takes this amount of time to ride a  bicycle if you don't ride a bicycle by  this date  we're going to put you up for adoption  but rather it's a process  you hold the seat you put the training  wheels on you take one wheel off and you  fumble through it and they fall and they  get back on  and you believe in the process and  eventually every kid can ride a bicycle  is that more of what it's like yeah i  think so  i'm big on both trust the process and  trust the people  so you have to have the process first if  you just start extending second chances  without a process  as to how that should look that's going  to fail always so trust the process  trust the people i'm a father of five  daughters i'm now the grandfather of  five  grandkids two more being born in the  next couple months  and what i found in my process i'm much  more tolerant of the second generation's  failures than i was the first  generation's failures  so it's also a process for us for some  of us it's  really hard to give second or third or  fifteenth chances  but the longer we do that the more adept  we become at that  and then we start to understand that it  is a longer journey than we want it to  be intuitively one of them to hurry up  and grab it and get  it but it is a long slow process and  unless we're willing to walk with people  that have had difficulty in life  they're probably going to crash and burn  at some point because nobody was there  to prop  them up for that next chance tell me a  story of someone  who has gone through your process 